2. What is the task of quantum communication?
a) Broadcasting satellite signals;  
b) Solving the key distribution problem  
c) Increasing the transmission power of a laser channel to overcome atmospheric losses (this is an engineering goal, not the core task of quantum communication).  
d) Performing conventional public‑key encryption (e.g., RSA) directly “inside” an optical fiber using photon pulses (quantum communication is not the same as classical encryption execution).

6. What is photon polarization?
a) Photon charge;  
b) Photon speed;  
c) Orientation of the electromagnetic field strength vector;  
d) The amount of energy stored in a photon due to its intensity (polarization is not an energy/intensity measure).

9. What bases are most often used in QKD protocols?
a) Vertical-horizontal and diagonal (0°-90° and 45°-135°);  
b) “Amplitude” and “frequency” bases (these are not polarization measurement bases used in common QKD like BB84).  
c) Left‑handed vs right‑handed “spin” bases defined by photon mass states (photons are massless; this framing is incorrect for QKD polarization bases).  
d) “Red vs blue color” bases (wavelength/color choice is not the standard pair of conjugate polarization bases in typical QKD protocols).

10. What happens if the recipient measures the polarization of the photon in the wrong basis?
a) With a probability of 0.5 the bit will be measured correctly;  
b) Will always be measured correctly;  
c) Will always be measured incorrectly;  
d) The photon will deterministically split into two photons, yielding two identical bits and revealing the sender’s basis (this does not happen; it is an invalid physical outcome).
